DEEP[41]=('The Puruṣa Sūkta (Ṛg Veda 10.90.12) is the oldest text describing the four orders, as arising from the mouth, arms, thighs and feet of the cosmic Person. The Gītā links them instead to <i>svabhāva</i> and the guṇas.',
 'This verse, like 4.13, is central to an old and ongoing debate. Śaṅkara and Rāmānuja generally read varṇa as fixed by birth, which they explained as the result of past karma. Many modern teachers, including Vivekananda, Aurobindo and Gandhi in different ways, emphasised nature and aptitude, and opposed discrimination by birth. Ambedkar rejected the varṇa system outright as unjust. We present these readings without taking a side. Verses 46–48 shift the focus to the dignity of every kind of work.',
 'Each person has a nature and gifts. Work that fits them is part of a good and peaceful life.')
DEEP[42]=('The qualities listed match the marks of knowledge in 13.7–11 and the divine qualities of 16.1–3.',
 'Commentators note that every item is an inner quality, not a privilege. Śaṅkara says these come naturally to one in whom sattva predominates. The epic itself questions birth-based status. In the Vana Parva, a serpent asks Yudhiṣṭhira what makes a brāhmaṇa. He answers truth, generosity, forgiveness, good conduct, gentleness, austerity and compassion. If these are found in a śūdra, he says, that person is a brāhmaṇa; if absent in one born a brāhmaṇa, he is not.',
 'Calm, honesty and wisdom are open to everyone to cultivate.')
DEEP[43]=('<i>Yuddhe cāpy apalāyanam</i>, “not fleeing in battle”, is exactly the duty Arjuna was about to abandon in chapter 1.',
 'Commentators see Kṛṣṇa gently reminding Arjuna of his own nature. Śaṅkara notes <i>īśvara-bhāva</i>, the spirit of leadership, and <i>dāna</i>, generosity, as kṣatriya qualities. The Mahābhārata praises kings who protect the weak and give freely, such as Śibi, who gave his own flesh to save a dove. In modern terms, the verse describes anyone whose calling is to protect: soldiers, police, leaders, guardians.',
 'Courage and generosity are the marks of a protector. Where you protect others, practise both.')
DEEP[44]=('Agriculture, cattle-rearing and trade (<i>kṛṣi, go-rakṣya, vāṇijya</i>) were the economic base of ancient society. <i>Paricaryā</i>, service, includes crafts and labour.',
 'Commentators note that the next two verses (45–46) immediately say that every kind of work, done as worship, leads to perfection. Many bhakti saints came from working communities: Kabīr was a weaver, Ravidās a cobbler, Gorā Kumbhār a potter, Sena a barber, Nandanār a farm labourer. Their lives showed that the Lord is reached through any honest work. Gandhi stressed the dignity of manual labour from this teaching.',
 'Every honest job is sacred when done well and offered with love.')
DEEP[45]=('',
 'Commentators say this verse answers a question many seekers have: must I change my life to find God? The Gītā says no; perfection comes through one’s own work. Śaṅkara says such work purifies the mind for knowledge. Rāmānuja says it becomes worship of the Lord. The next verse explains how.',
 'Your own work, done wholeheartedly, can be your spiritual path. You need not look elsewhere.')
DEEP[46]=('<i>Sva-karmaṇā tam abhyarcya</i>, “worshipping him through one’s own work”: <i>arcanā</i> is the word for temple worship with flowers and lamps.',
 'The image is striking: daily work becomes the flowers offered to the Lord. Commentators of every school quote this verse. Vivekananda taught “work is worship”, and Gandhi spoke of bread labour as sacred. In the Mahābhārata, a hunter named Dharmavyādha teaches dharma to a proud brahmin. He says he gained wisdom by doing his humble work faithfully and caring for his parents.',
 'Offer your work as worship. Then every working day becomes a prayer.')
DEEP[47]=('This verse repeats 3.35, adding <i>svabhāva-niyatam</i>, “ordained by one’s own nature”.',
 'Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa ends where he began on this theme. Arjuna, a warrior, was drawn to the life of a beggar-monk; his true path was his own duty. Śaṅkara says acting by one’s nature brings no sin because it is natural. Rāmānuja says it is easier and safer than another’s path. Modern readers often take it as advice about vocation: find the work that fits you, rather than copying others.',
 'Be yourself. Imitation, however polished, is risky and tiring.')
DEEP[48]=('<i>Dhūmenāgnir ivāvṛtāḥ</i>, “as fire is covered by smoke”: an image from 3.38, now used differently.',
 'Commentators find this one of the most realistic verses in the Gītā. Every action involves some harm or fault: the farmer kills insects, the doctor causes pain, the judge disappoints someone. Śaṅkara says no action is wholly free of defect, so one should not abandon one’s duty for that reason. Rāmānuja says the defects are removed by offering the work to the Lord. For Arjuna, the violence of war is real, but abandoning a just duty is worse.',
 'Your work isn’t perfect; no one’s is. Do it well and offer it, smoke and all.')
DEEP[49]=('<i>Naiṣkarmya-siddhi</i>, the perfection of freedom from action, is the title of a famous work by Sureśvara, Śaṅkara’s disciple.',
 'Commentators note that 3.4 said naiṣkarmya is not reached by not acting. Here it is reached through renunciation after long unattached work. Śaṅkara says this is the state of the knower who has given up all action in knowledge. Rāmānuja says it is the state where action no longer binds. The verse begins the final ascent of 18.50–56.',
 'Freedom is the fruit of long, unattached work. Be patient with the process.')
DEEP[50]=('',
 'Commentators see 18.50–56 as a summary of the whole Gītā’s path in seven verses. It moves from purified understanding and self-control, through meditation and dispassion, to becoming Brahman, and then to supreme devotion. Śaṅkara reads the stages as leading to knowledge of Brahman. Rāmānuja reads them as leading to devotion. Both read the same verses as a ladder.',
 'Every path has a summit. These verses are a map of it. Read them slowly.')
DEEP[51]=('This verse uses the sāttvika understanding (18.30) and sāttvika resolve (18.33) just described.',
 'Commentators note the practical steps: a clear mind, steady will, giving up sense distractions, and casting off likes and dislikes. Śaṅkara says these are the means to knowledge. Rāmānuja says they prepare the mind for the vision of the self. The sentence runs through 18.53, describing the person ready for Brahman.',
 'Clear thinking, steady will, and freedom from likes and dislikes: build on these.')
DEEP[52]=('<i>Vivikta-sevī</i>, one who resorts to solitude, and <i>laghv-āśī</i>, one who eats lightly, echo the meditation instructions of chapter 6.',
 'Commentators note that these describe the life of one approaching the goal, whether outwardly a monk or a householder. Śaṅkara says solitude helps concentration. Rāmānuja says light eating keeps the mind clear. Many modern teachers recommend some daily solitude, mindful eating and meditation as the core of a balanced inner life.',
 'A little solitude, light eating and daily meditation can change a life.')
DEEP[53]=('The six faults given up here, ego, force, pride, desire, anger and possessiveness, are almost exactly those of the demonic nature in 16.18.',
 'Commentators note the journey from chapter 16 to chapter 18: the faults that bind are now let go. <i>Nirmama</i>, free from “mine”, recalls 2.71. <i>Brahma-bhūyāya kalpate</i>, fit for becoming Brahman, recalls 14.26. Śaṅkara says such a person is ready for liberation. Rāmānuja says such a person becomes like Brahman in purity.',
 'Peace grows as “I” and “mine” shrink. Let go of one small claim today.')
DEEP[54]=('<i>Brahma-bhūtaḥ prasannātmā</i> is one of the most quoted phrases in the Gītā, describing the serene, realised person.',
 'This verse is a meeting point of the schools. Śaṅkara reads <i>parā bhakti</i> as the highest knowledge, which is love of the Self. Rāmānuja reads it as the supreme love that follows self-realisation and leads to the Lord. Madhva stresses that even the freed soul worships the Lord. Śaṅkara himself composed many devotional hymns, such as the Bhaja Govindam, showing that the knower can be a devotee.',
 'At the summit, knowledge and love are one. Don’t set them against each other.')
DEEP[55]=('',
 'Commentators note that this completes 11.54: by undivided devotion the Lord is known, seen and entered. Śaṅkara reads “entering” as oneness with the Lord. Rāmānuja reads it as reaching and enjoying him. Madhva reads it as entering his presence. The verse shows the Gītā’s circle: knowledge leads to love, and love leads to deeper knowledge.',
 'Love leads to knowing, and knowing deepens love. Let them grow together.')
DEEP[56]=('',
 'Commentators find great comfort here for householders. One need not leave work or family; refuge and grace are enough. Rāmānuja’s school treasures this verse as a promise of grace to those who depend on the Lord. Śaṅkara says the grace of the Lord brings the eternal state. The word <i>prasāda</i>, grace, returns in 18.58, 18.62 and 18.73.',
 'You can reach the highest while doing your daily work, with the Divine as your refuge.')
DEEP[57]=('<i>Buddhi-yoga</i>, the yoga of understanding, was first named in 2.49 and returned in 10.10.',
 'Commentators see this as Kṛṣṇa’s instruction to Arjuna in a single verse, gathering 3.30, 9.27 and 12.6. Śaṅkara says <i>cetasā</i>, “mentally”, means with discrimination. Rāmānuja says it means offering every action to the Lord. The four elements, offering, aiming, understanding and remembering, are the whole practice in brief.',
 'Offer it all, keep the highest aim, and remember. That is the whole method in one line.')
DEEP[58]=('This is one of the rare places where Kṛṣṇa speaks with a warning: <i>vinaṅkṣyasi</i>, “you will perish”.',
 'Commentators say this is not a threat but the honest care of a teacher. Śaṅkara says the ruin comes from ego, not from the Lord. Rāmānuja says the Lord warns out of love. Many traditions speak of grace freely given and of the ego that blocks it. The Gītā’s balance is clear: grace carries, but one must not refuse it.',
 'Grace is near; ego is the only wall. Lower the wall a little each day.')
DEEP[59]=('Arjuna’s <i>na yotsye</i>, “I will not fight” (2.9), is quoted back to him almost word for word.',
 'Commentators note Kṛṣṇa’s directness. Arjuna’s decision not to fight came from ego and confusion, not real renunciation. His warrior nature will reassert itself. Śaṅkara says nature, formed by past karma, will compel him. Rāmānuja says it is better to act knowingly than to be dragged. The verse links to 3.33: beings follow their nature; repression will not hold.',
 'You can’t run from who you are for long. Act from your nature, wisely.')
DEEP[60]=('',
 'Commentators note that the choice is not whether to act, but how. Śaṅkara says one bound by nature will act anyway; better to act with knowledge. Rāmānuja says better to do willingly what the Lord wishes. The Mahābhārata confirms the prophecy: Arjuna does fight, and decides the war.',
 'Since you will act anyway, act with understanding and good intent.')
DEEP[61]=('The image of beings mounted on a <i>yantra</i>, a machine, may refer to a puppet stage, a potter’s wheel or a water-wheel.',
 'Commentators find this verse deep and sobering. Beings are turned by karma and nature, with the Lord seated in the heart. Śaṅkara says the Lord as inner controller moves beings according to their karma by his māyā. Rāmānuja says the Lord within directs all, which is why refuge in him (18.62) is the way out. The Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (3.7) describes the <i>antaryāmin</i>, the inner controller, dwelling in all.',
 'The one who turns the wheel lives in your heart. Turn toward him rather than struggling alone.')
DEEP[62]=('',
 'Commentators of every school see refuge, <i>śaraṇāgati</i>, as central here, leading to 18.66. Śaṅkara says refuge means taking the Lord as one’s Self. Rāmānuja says it means complete dependence. Madhva says it means complete devotion. The promise is <i>parāṃ śāntim</i>, supreme peace, the same peace promised in 2.71 and 4.39.',
 'Give your whole self in trust. Peace follows.')
DEEP[63]=('',
 'Commentators admire this verse greatly. After seventeen chapters of teaching, the Lord does not command; he invites reflection and leaves the choice to Arjuna. Śaṅkara says the Lord has given all that is needed, and now Arjuna must decide. Rāmānuja says the Lord respects the freedom of the self. Many modern readers see here a deep respect for human freedom and reason.',
 'Good teachers don’t force. They teach, then trust you to choose.')
DEEP[64]=('',
 'Commentators note the sequence: freedom given (18.63), then love expressed (18.64), then the final teaching (18.65–66). Śaṅkara says the Lord speaks for Arjuna’s good. Rāmānuja’s tradition sees this as the Lord’s overflowing love, <i>vātsalya</i>. The teaching that follows is the most important of all, and it comes wrapped in affection.',
 'The deepest teaching is given in love. Teach and correct others that way too.')
DEEP[65]=('This verse repeats 9.34 with the addition of a solemn promise.',
 'Commentators note that the Lord now binds himself by oath: <i>satyaṃ te pratijāne</i>, “I truly promise you”. Śaṅkara says the promise removes all doubt. Rāmānuja says the Lord pledges himself to the devotee. Madhva stresses total devotion. The verse and the next are often printed together as the climax of the Gītā.',
 'You are promised a homecoming if you turn your heart. Trust the promise.')
DEEP[66]=('This verse is called the <i>carama śloka</i>, the final verse, in the Śrī Vaiṣṇava tradition, which counts it among its three great mantras.',
 '<i>Mā śucaḥ</i>, do not grieve, answers Arjuna’s grief of 1.47 and 2.1. It recalls 2.11, where the teaching began, and 16.5. Madhusūdana reads it as giving up reliance on one’s own efforts. Gandhi read it as total self-surrender to truth. The verse has comforted countless people in despair. Whatever the reading, its last words are the same: do not grieve.',
 'When your own efforts fall short, let go and trust. Do not grieve.')
DEEP[67]=('Some commentators read this verse as protecting the student as well as the teaching: forcing deep truths on the unready can harm their faith (3.26).',
 'Commentators explain the four kinds who should not be taught: those without discipline, without devotion, without desire to hear, and those who mock. Śaṅkara says the teaching is not for those who would misuse or scorn it. Rāmānuja says it is out of respect for the teaching, not exclusivity. Similar advice appears in many traditions: “do not cast pearls before swine”.',
 'Share wisdom with those who want it. Don’t force it on the unwilling.')
DEEP[68]=('The Gītā is unusual among scriptures in praising those who teach it as among the dearest to God.',
 'Commentators note the condition: teaching <i>mad-bhakteṣu</i>, to devotees, with devotion oneself. Śaṅkara says such a teacher reaches the Lord. Rāmānuja says teaching is a form of devotion. The history of the Gītā is a history of teachers: Śaṅkara, Rāmānuja, Madhva, Jñāneśvar, Vivekananda, Tilak, Gandhi and countless household teachers.',
 'Share what has helped you with those who are seeking. That too is worship.')
DEEP[69]=('',
 'Commentators say spreading wisdom with love pleases the Lord more than any ritual, because it helps many beings. Śaṅkara says no one is dearer than the teacher of the Gītā. Rāmānuja agrees. The verse has inspired many to teach Gītā classes in homes, schools and temples. It also encourages humble sharing: one need not be a scholar to pass on what one has learned.',
 'Helping others understand is the dearest service. Share one insight today.')
DEEP[70]=('<i>Jñāna-yajña</i>, the sacrifice of knowledge, was praised in 4.33 as the best of all sacrifices.',
 'Commentators say studying the Gītā is itself worship. Śaṅkara says study is a sacrifice of knowledge because it brings understanding. Rāmānuja says it pleases the Lord as worship does. Gītā Jayantī, the day of the Gītā’s teaching, is celebrated by reading it in full, and many people read a chapter a day as a spiritual practice.',
 'Reading these pages is itself an offering. Treat it so.')
DEEP[71]=('<i>Śraddhāvān anasūyaḥ</i>, “with faith and without fault-finding”: the same two qualities asked of Arjuna in 3.31 and 9.1.',
 'Commentators note the generous promise: you do not have to be a scholar to benefit. Śaṅkara says even the one who only hears is freed from sins and reaches the worlds of the righteous. Rāmānuja says hearing with faith is the first step toward devotion. Many traditions have honoured listening: <i>śravaṇa</i>, hearing, is the first of the nine forms of devotion listed in the Bhāgavata (7.5.23).',
 'Simply listening with an open heart counts. Keep listening.')
DEEP[72]=('',
 'Commentators note the teaching method: after teaching, the teacher asks. Śaṅkara says Kṛṣṇa asks so that Arjuna will reflect and confirm. Rāmānuja says the Lord wants to be sure the delusion of chapter 1 has gone. Good teachers in every field do the same: they check understanding instead of assuming it.',
 'Check in with yourself after learning something: has the confusion really lifted?')
DEEP[73]=('These are Arjuna’s last words in the Gītā. He uses the name <i>Acyuta</i>, “the unfallen”, the same name he used in 1.21 when he asked Kṛṣṇa to drive the chariot between the armies.',
 'Commentators see the whole Gītā in this verse. <i>Naṣṭo mohaḥ</i>, delusion gone, answers 2.2. <i>Smṛtir labdhā</i>, memory regained, reverses 2.63, where anger destroyed memory. <i>Sthito’smi</i>, I stand firm, answers 1.47, where Arjuna sank down. <i>Kariṣye vacanaṃ tava</i> answers 2.9. Śaṅkara says the memory regained is the memory of the Self. Rāmānuja says it is the memory of the Lord and of one’s duty.',
 'Wisdom is often remembering what we always were. Take time to remember.')
DEEP[74]=('Sañjaya speaks to Dhṛtarāṣṭra, who started the Gītā with his anxious question in 1.1.',
 'Commentators note that the frame closes. The blind king asked what his sons were doing; Sañjaya answers with a dialogue that has nothing to do with winning and everything to do with truth. Śaṅkara does not comment much on these closing verses, but later teachers see Sañjaya’s wonder as the right response to the Gītā. <i>Roma-harṣaṇa</i>, hair-raising, recalls Arjuna’s state in 1.29 and 11.14.',
 'Some words still move us after thousands of years. Let them move you.')
DEEP[75]=('Vyāsa gave Sañjaya divine sight before the war, so that he could narrate it to the blind king (Bhīṣma Parva 2).',
 'Commentators note that even the hearing of the Gītā depends on a teacher’s grace. Sañjaya, a minister, became a channel of the highest teaching. Śaṅkara’s tradition honours Vyāsa as the guru of gurus, and Guru Pūrṇimā is also called Vyāsa Pūrṇimā. The verse reminds every reader that wisdom reaches us through a chain of teachers.',
 'We receive wisdom through those who pass it on. Be grateful to your teachers.')
DEEP[76]=('',
 'Commentators note that the Gītā rewards rereading. Śaṅkara says the dialogue is holy because it removes sin. Rāmānuja says Sañjaya’s joy is that of a devotee recalling the Lord. Many people find new meaning each time they read it; Gandhi called the Gītā his mother, to whom he turned again and again.',
 'Return to what uplifts you, again and again. Each reading brings something new.')
DEEP[77]=('Sañjaya too saw the cosmic form of chapter 11, through Vyāsa’s gift.',
 'Commentators note that Sañjaya both heard the teaching and saw the vision. His wonder is meant to spark ours. Śaṅkara says his repeated joy shows the greatness of what he witnessed. Rāmānuja says the vision of Hari’s form fills him with love. <i>Vismaya</i>, wonder, is one of the classic moods of Indian aesthetics, <i>adbhuta rasa</i>.',
 'Keep a sense of wonder. It keeps faith alive and the heart young.')
DEEP[78]=('This is the last verse of the Gītā. In the epic, the war that follows lasts eighteen days, and the Pāṇḍavas win.',
 'Commentators read the verse at two levels. In the story, it is Sañjaya’s last warning to the blind king: with Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna together, the Pāṇḍavas cannot lose. Universally, it is a formula: where divine wisdom (Kṛṣṇa, the Lord of yoga) and dedicated human effort (Arjuna, the archer) join, there is fortune, victory, well-being and sound policy. Some note that the Gītā’s first word is <i>dharma</i> and its last is <i>mama</i>. Its story moves from <i>māmakāḥ</i>, “mine”, to true dharma.',
 'Join your effort to the highest wisdom you know. That is the formula for every victory worth having.')
